> Damien Neil wrote:
> > A good scoring system should:
> > - Have a theoretical maximum score that is impossible to reach in
> > practice.
> 
> Why?

One of the problems with the current system is that repeating certain
actions can give an arbitrarily high score.  To prevent this, you need
to limit the number of points that can be earned from any given source.

The point of the score, however, is to allow comparisons between games. 
If the maximum can be reached in practice, there's no way to compare
two games which reach it.


> > - A fixed point award for each new level reached, with bonuses for
> > certain milestones.
> 
> This would make the protection racket impossible, since you would never
> be able to reach Mine Town without accumulating levels...

I think you're confusing score and EXP.  Or am I missing something?


> > - Exit multiplier--your final score is multiplied by a constant, based
> > on how you ended the game.  Ascention gives the best multiplier, while
> > quitting gives the worst.
> 
> Note that currently, quitting gives a *bonus*, as opposed to just dying.
> It's a reward for the gamble you take in not dealing out that last blow,
> but giving up instead. After all, had you not quit, you might have
> survived.

Interesting.  I didn't know that.


